Insecure Deserialization
Insecure Deserialization is a vulnerability where an application deserializes untrusted data without proper validation, allowing attackers to manipulate objects and potentially execute malicious code on the server. 
This vulnerability can lead to:

Remote Code Execution (RCE)
Privilege Escalation
Authentication Bypass
Data Manipulation
Denial of Service
In many cases, insecure deserialization becomes one of the most dangerous vulnerabilities, because it allows attackers to inject malicious objects directly into the application's runtime.


1. What is Serialization?
Serialization is the process of converting an in-memory object into a format that can be stored or transmitted.
Example formats:

Binary streams
JSON
XML
YAML
Custom encoded formats
Example:

Object in memory
      │
      ▼
Serialized into bytes
      │
      ▼
Stored in file / database / cookie / API
Example in Java:

ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(fileStream);
out.writeObject(userObject);
Here, the object is converted into a byte stream representation.


2. What is Deserialization?
Deserialization is the reverse process.
It reconstructs the object from serialized data.
Example:

Serialized bytes
       │
       ▼
Deserialization
       │
       ▼
Object reconstructed in memory
Java example:

ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);
Object obj = in.readObject();
This recreates the object in memory. 


