5. Why Insecure Deserialization Is Dangerous
The key problem:

Deserialization recreates objects
Objects may execute code automatically
Some programming languages allow special methods that run automatically during deserialization.
Examples:

Java → readObject()
Python → __reduce__()
PHP → __wakeup()
.NET → OnDeserialized()
If attackers control serialized data, they can trigger these methods.


6. Basic Insecure Deserialization Flow

Attacker crafts malicious serialized object
               │
               ▼
Application receives object
               │
               ▼
Application deserializes object
               │
               ▼
Malicious code executes
This can result in:

Command execution
File access
System compromise


7. Simple Python Example
Vulnerable code:

import pickle
from flask import request

data = request.get_data()
obj = pickle.loads(data)
Here the server directly deserializes user input.
If an attacker sends a malicious payload, arbitrary code may execute. 


8. Java Example
Vulnerable code:

ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(request.getInputStream());
Object obj = in.readObject();
The application reconstructs objects directly from user-controlled input.
If a malicious serialized object is provided, it may trigger dangerous code execution. 


