Exploiting Insecure Deserialization
To exploit insecure deserialization, an attacker must be able to control the serialized data that the application deserializes.
Typical attacker-controlled entry points include:

Cookies
HTTP request body
Hidden form fields
File uploads
API payloads
Message queues
Once an attacker can modify serialized data, they can attempt to inject malicious objects.


Typical Exploitation Flow

Step 1: Attacker identifies serialized data
Step 2: Attacker decodes or reverse engineers the format
Step 3: Attacker modifies the serialized object
Step 4: Attacker injects malicious object payload
Step 5: Application deserializes the object
Step 6: Malicious code executes
