Understanding Gadget Chains
One of the most important concepts in deserialization exploitation is the gadget chain.
A gadget chain is:

A sequence of existing classes and methods
that can be abused to perform malicious actions
during deserialization.
Attackers do not usually inject new code.
Instead, they reuse existing classes in the application or libraries.
Example:

Class A → calls method in Class B
Class B → triggers method in Class C
Class C → executes command
Diagram:

Serialized Object
      │
      ▼
Class A
      │
      ▼
Class B
      │
      ▼
Class C
      │
      ▼
Command Execution
These chains are called POP Chains (Property Oriented Programming).


POP Chains (Property-Oriented Programming)
POP chains exploit how object properties trigger method execution during deserialization.
Example concept:

Attacker sets object property
     │
     ▼
Property triggers method
     │
     ▼
Method calls another method
     │
     ▼
Chain continues
     │
     ▼
Dangerous operation executed
This can lead to:

File write
Command execution
Network requests
Privilege escalation


