Detecting Insecure Deserialization
Detecting deserialization vulnerabilities requires identifying where untrusted serialized data is processed by the application.
Typical indicators include:

Large encoded values in cookies
Serialized objects in request bodies
Binary blobs passed between services
Encoded object structures in hidden fields
Common serialization formats attackers encounter:

Java Serialization
JSON-based object serialization
XML serialization
Python Pickle
PHP serialized objects
.NET BinaryFormatter


Serialized Object Indicators
Certain signatures can reveal serialized objects.
Java Serialization Signature
Java serialized objects often start with:

AC ED 00 05
Example encoded form:

rO0ABXNyABFqYXZh...
This is frequently seen in:

Session cookies
Hidden fields
API payloads


PHP Serialized Objects
Example PHP serialized object:

O:8:"UserData":2:{s:4:"name";s:5:"admin";s:4:"role";s:5:"super";}
Structure:

O = Object
8 = class name length
UserData = class
Attackers can modify these values.


Python Pickle Indicators
Pickle payloads often appear as binary blobs.
Example:

gASVHgAAAAAAAAB9lCiMBG5hbWWUjAVhZG1p...
If user input reaches pickle.loads(), it may be exploitable.


