Secure Architecture Principles
Preventing deserialization attacks begins at the architecture level.
The safest approach:

Never deserialize untrusted data.
If deserialization must be used:

Restrict allowed object types
Validate input sources
Use safe serializers
Isolate deserialization components
Architecture diagram:

Client Input
     │
     ▼
Validation Layer
     │
     ▼
Safe Serialization Format
     │
     ▼
Application Logic
Never allow:

Direct user input → object deserialization


Secure Design Strategy
A safer design pattern is data-only serialization.
Instead of serializing objects:

Serialize plain data structures
Example:
Unsafe:

Serialized Java object
Safe:

JSON data structure
Example JSON:

{
 "userId": 123,
 "role": "user"
}
This removes object behavior during deserialization.


Secure Coding Patterns
Java – Unsafe Example

ObjectInputStream ois =
    new ObjectInputStream(request.getInputStream());

User user = (User) ois.readObject();
Problem:

User input directly deserialized
This allows attackers to inject malicious objects.


Java – Safer Pattern
Restrict allowed classes.
Example:

ObjectInputFilter filter =
    ObjectInputFilter.Config.createFilter("com.myapp.User");

ObjectInputStream ois =
    new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);

ois.setObjectInputFilter(filter);
Only trusted classes can be deserialized.


Python – Unsafe Example

import pickle

data = request.data
user = pickle.loads(data)
Problem:

pickle.loads executes arbitrary code


Python – Safer Pattern
Use JSON instead.

import json

data = json.loads(request.data)
JSON only restores data, not executable objects.


Defensive Security Controls
Strong defense includes multiple layers.
1. Avoid Native Serialization
Prefer:

JSON
Protocol Buffers
MessagePack
These formats do not execute object behavior.


2. Integrity Protection
Serialized data should be protected using:

HMAC signatures
Digital signatures
Encryption
Example:

SerializedObject + HMAC
If modified → request rejected.


3. Class Allowlisting
Allow only safe classes.

Allowed classes list
Reject unknown object types.


4. Isolation
Run deserialization logic in:

Sandbox environments
Containers
Restricted services
Limit potential damage.


